:: ReadingMap [overview] {"title":"Reading map","group":"Start here","order":70}
@TopicHeader({
  eyebrow: "Orientation · no research results",
  title: "A two-paper reading map",
  summary: "Use the same vocabulary while keeping modeling levels and claims explicit."
})

## Paper A: computational / agent-based lens

Review these concepts first:

- bounded-confidence eligibility and update timing;
- directed follower networks and rewiring;
- content-based versus topology-based exposure;
- the distinction between polarization and segregation;
- trajectories versus end states.

## Paper B: mesoscopic / mathematical lens

Add these concepts:

- node densities and edge-conditioned densities;
- finite-step transition kernels and gain–loss equations;
- closures, moment approximations, and their validity regimes;
- Markov generators, absorbing sets, and committors;
- validation against the observable actually being predicted.

::: callout {tone="scope" title="Deliberate omission"}
This map does not name model variants, derived operators, experimental configurations, numerical findings, or proposed mechanisms from either paper.
:::

## Implementation tour

This example also serves as a gneh feature map:

- passages, metadata, links, lists, styled containers, and authored views live in `story.inkdown`;
- `Formula.mjs` uses `defineView()` to emit semantic LaTeX nodes;
- `latex-plugin.mjs` is an application-owned `renderer-dom` plugin backed by KaTeX;
- `ModelLab.mjs` is a handwritten `definePassage()` with state, lifecycle, regions, and actions;
- `main.mjs` uses `definePassages()` to compose native and compiled passages, then mounts them beside ordinary application chrome;
- the host adds search, progress, undo, and local save/load without putting DOM authority in story source.

